LAST-MODIFIED:20260623T210316Z
UID:10006740-1784221200-1784235600@www.ashevilleart.org
SUMMARY:Third Thursday
DESCRIPTION:Every Third Thursday\, the Museum comes alive with an evening filled with music\, tours\, art making\, special cocktails in our rooftop café\, and more! \nThis month we are featuring a Curator’s Talk | Hidden in Plain View – Art in Southern Appalachia\, with co-creator of Warp\, Weft\, and the Grid\, Kathleen Curtis Wilson from 6–7pm. \nRESERVE TICKETS NOW
URL:https://www.ashevilleart.org/event/third-thursday-11/
LOCATION:Asheville Art Museum\, 2 South Pack Square\, Asheville\, NC\, 28801\, United States
CATEGORIES:Third Thursday
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://www.ashevilleart.org/wp-content/uploads/rooftop-sunset-scaled-1.jpg
GEO:35.5946714;-82.5512827
X-APPLE-STRUCTURED-LOCATION;VALUE=URI;X-ADDRESS=Asheville Art Museum 2 South Pack Square Asheville NC 28801 United States;X-APPLE-RADIUS=500;X-TITLE=2 South Pack Square:geo:-82.5512827,35.5946714
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Ashevilleart": "https://www.ashevilleart.org/event/curators-talk-a-distinctive-artistic-tradition/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:ashevilleart
X-SOURCE:Asheville Art Museum
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260716T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260716T190000
DTSTAMP:20260624T194325Z
CREATED:20260507T155909Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260624T194325Z
UID:10006611-1784224800-1784228400@www.ashevilleart.org
SUMMARY:Curator's Talk | Hidden in Plain View—Art in Southern Appalachia
DESCRIPTION:Co-curator of Warp\, Weft\, and the Grid\, Kathleen Curtis Wilson \nCurator Kathleen Curtis Wilson will present a series of photographs and share personal stories about her collection of bed coverlets woven by women in southern Appalachia\, 1850–1930. An appreciation of Appalachian weaving was hindered by stereotypical images of Appalachia as a poverty-ridden\, art-poor region. These images and a lack of women’s material culture studies in the region once made it easy to dismiss Appalachia as void of objects of art\, creativity\, and design worthy of national attention. \nToday\, graphic artists and laypersons will appreciate the simple designs of early coverlet patterns for the straight lines\, accuracy\, and bold color choices of this distinctive artistic tradition. \nThe woven coverlets images presented during Wilson’s presentation make it apparent that many southern Appalachian weavers did not put away their looms in the nineteenth century but made a conscious decision to continue to weave and preserve beautiful objects for their own pleasure\, as gifts for family members\, or in celebration of important events. \nKathleen Curtis Wilson is the author of five non-fiction books: The Quiet Work of Women (2001\, reprinting 2026); Uplifting the South—Mary Mildred Sullivan’s Legacy for Appalachia (2005); Irish People\, Irish Linen (2011); Dancing at the Warm Springs Hotel (2022); and Adventurous Max Flax (2023)\, an educational children’s book. Her first novel\, The Fabric of Wishful Thinking. (available 2026). \nWilson served as honorary fellow at both University of Ulster\, Northern Ireland\, and Virginia Humanities\, Charlottesville\, Virginia. She has given scores of public speeches and lectures at professional conferences in the United States and abroad. Born in Michigan\, Wilson studied weaving at The Cranbrook Schools. She established and operated Old Abingdon Weavers in Southwest Virginia from 1983–1993. She lives in Blacksburg\, Virginia.
URL:https://www.ashevilleart.org/event/curators-talk-a-distinctive-artistic-tradition/
LOCATION:Asheville Art Museum\, 2 South Pack Square\, Asheville\, NC\, 28801\, United States
CATEGORIES:In Conversation
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://www.ashevilleart.org/wp-content/uploads/Kathleen-Curators-Talk-7.16.26-1.png
GEO:35.5946714;-82.5512827
X-APPLE-STRUCTURED-LOCATION;VALUE=URI;X-ADDRESS=Asheville Art Museum 2 South Pack Square Asheville NC 28801 United States;X-APPLE-RADIUS=500;X-TITLE=2 South Pack Square:geo:-82.5512827,35.5946714
END:VEVENT
